self.RULES_HTML = "<h2>Cordata: rules</h2>\n<p><em>Chess on a 5×8 board with pawn batteries.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Version 1.1, 2026-11-14.</strong> Compared with version 1.0, the starting position changes (eight pawns a side, section 2) and the hitch is added (4.6). The points marked <strong>[D11]</strong>, <strong>[D12]</strong>, <strong>[D13]</strong> are interpretations not yet confirmed by the author, collected in section 7.</p>\n<h3>1. Board and pieces</h3>\n<p id=\"r1.1\"><span class=\"n\">1.1.</span> The board has 5 files and 8 ranks (40 squares). The files are called <code>a</code>–<code>e</code> from left to right as White sees them; the ranks are numbered <code>1</code>–<code>8</code> starting from White's side.</p>\n<p id=\"r1.2\"><span class=\"n\">1.2.</span> Each player has 13 pieces: 1 king, 2 rooks, 2 knights, 8 pawns. There are no queens and no bishops.</p>\n<p id=\"r1.3\"><span class=\"n\">1.3.</span> \"Forward\" means towards the higher ranks for White and towards the lower ranks for Black. The \"last rank\" is the eighth for White and the first for Black.</p>\n<h3>2. Starting position</h3>\n<pre>8   r  n  k  n  r      Black (lower case)\n7   p  p  p  p  p\n6   p  .  p  .  p\n5   .  .  .  .  .\n4   .  .  .  .  .\n3   P  .  P  .  P\n2   P  P  P  P  P\n1   R  N  K  N  R      White (upper case)\n    a  b  c  d  e</pre>\n<p>R = rook, N = knight, K = king, P = pawn.</p>\n<p id=\"r2.1\"><span class=\"n\">2.1.</span> White: rooks on a1 and e1, knights on b1 and d1, king on c1, pawns on a2–e2 and on a3, c3, e3.</p>\n<p id=\"r2.2\"><span class=\"n\">2.2.</span> Black: rooks on a8 and e8, knights on b8 and d8, king on c8, pawns on a7–e7 and on a6, c6, e6.</p>\n<p id=\"r2.3\"><span class=\"n\">2.3.</span> Each player therefore starts with three batteries (section 5), on the a-, c- and e-files. At the start the knights have no move: the squares they could go to are occupied by their own pawns.</p>\n<h3>3. Play</h3>\n<p id=\"r3.1\"><span class=\"n\">3.1.</span> White moves first; then the players move in turn, one move each. Passing is not allowed.</p>\n<p id=\"r3.2\"><span class=\"n\">3.2.</span> King, rook and knight move and capture as in chess.</p>\n<p id=\"r3.3\"><span class=\"n\">3.3.</span> Any move after which one's own king is in check, that is attacked by an opposing piece, is illegal.</p>\n<p id=\"r3.4\"><span class=\"n\">3.4.</span> <strong>Exception to 3.3.</strong> A move that brings one of one's own pawns to the last rank is always legal, even if it leaves or puts one's own king in check: the game ends at once with the victory of the player who moved (6.1c). This holds both when the king is already in check before the move and when it comes into check as a result of the move (pinned pawn).</p>\n<p id=\"r3.5\"><span class=\"n\">3.5.</span> There is no castling.</p>\n<h3>4. Single pawn</h3>\n<p id=\"r4.1\"><span class=\"n\">4.1.</span> It advances one square forward, if the square is empty.</p>\n<p id=\"r4.2\"><span class=\"n\">4.2.</span> A white pawn on the second rank, or a black pawn on the seventh, may advance two squares, if both are empty. The pawns that start the game on the third and on the sixth rank do not have the double step. <strong>[D11]</strong></p>\n<p id=\"r4.3\"><span class=\"n\">4.3.</span> It captures one square diagonally forward. It does not capture straight ahead (except by the battery rule, section 5).</p>\n<p id=\"r4.4\"><span class=\"n\">4.4.</span> En passant capture as in chess: a pawn that has just advanced two squares may be captured, on the very next move, by an opposing pawn that attacks the square it passed over. Only the double step 4.2 allows an en passant capture; the block advance of a battery does not (5.8).</p>\n<p id=\"r4.5\"><span class=\"n\">4.5.</span> There is no promotion: a pawn that reaches the last rank wins the game (6.1c).</p>\n<p id=\"r4.6\"><span class=\"n\">4.6.</span> <strong>Hitch.</strong> A pawn may move one square diagonally forward to an empty square, without capturing, if the square it arrives on is, on its new file, immediately in front of or immediately behind another pawn of the same colour. After the hitch the two pawns form a battery (5.1). Examples for White: with pawns on b3 and c3, the pawn on b3 may go to c4 (in front of c3) and the pawn on c3 may go to b4 (in front of b3); with pawns on b3 and c5, the pawn on b3 may go to c4 (behind c5).</p>\n<ul><li>The hitch is not a capture and not a threat: the pawn still attacks diagonally only as in 4.3, and an opposing king may stand on the square where the hitch would arrive.</li><li>A hitch that arrives on the last rank wins the game (6.1c). <strong>[D12]</strong></li><li>On the en passant square the diagonal step is the en passant capture (4.4), not the hitch. <strong>[D13]</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>5. Pawn battery</h3>\n<p id=\"r5.1\"><span class=\"n\">5.1.</span> <strong>Definition.</strong> A battery is a group of two or more pawns of the same colour on consecutive squares of one file, taken at its full extent (neither in front of the group nor behind it is there another pawn of the same colour in contact with it). The most advanced pawn is the lead pawn.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.2\"><span class=\"n\">5.2.</span> <strong>Block advance.</strong> In a battery of <em>n</em> pawns the player chooses a number <em>k</em> with 2 ≤ <em>k</em> ≤ <em>n</em>; as a single move, the <em>k</em> most advanced pawns advance one square each. The other <em>n</em> − <em>k</em> pawns stay where they are. The move is allowed if the square in front of the lead pawn is</p>\n<ul><li>empty;</li><li>or occupied by an opposing piece of any kind, which is captured.</li></ul>\n<p>It is not allowed if that square is occupied by one of the player's own pieces. With three pawns in a column, therefore, one may advance with two or with all three. The rule holds for batteries of any length, and every block of two or more pawns captures straight ahead in the same way.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.3\"><span class=\"n\">5.3.</span> <strong>Advance of the lead pawn alone.</strong> The lead pawn may advance alone by one square according to 4.1: only if the square is empty, and without capturing.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.4\"><span class=\"n\">5.4.</span> <strong>Frontal attack.</strong> A battery attacks the square in front of its lead pawn. If the opposing king stands there it is check; the opposing king may not enter that square.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.5\"><span class=\"n\">5.5.</span> <strong>Formation.</strong> The battery exists from the moment 5.1 holds, however it came about: by the advance of a pawn (of one or two squares) until it touches another, by a hitch (4.6), by a diagonal capture that brings a pawn onto the file, by a block advance. The frontal attack 5.4 holds at once: one can give check, and checkmate, by forming a battery.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.6\"><span class=\"n\">5.6.</span> <strong>Diagonal moves.</strong> Every pawn of a battery keeps the diagonal capture 4.3, the en passant capture 4.4 and the hitch 4.6, like a single pawn.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.7\"><span class=\"n\">5.7.</span> <strong>Breaking up.</strong> When a pawn leaves the file (diagonal capture, en passant capture, hitch) or is captured (also en passant), the remaining pawns form a battery only if they still satisfy 5.1. For example, if the middle pawn is removed from a battery of three, two single pawns remain.</p>\n<p id=\"r5.8\"><span class=\"n\">5.8.</span> <strong>No double step for the battery.</strong> No pawn of a battery, except the lead pawn, may advance alone. The block advance is always of one square only, even when the last pawn of the battery stands on its own starting square. Example: the white battery b2–b3 advances to b3–b4 and no further. Neither pawn has made a double step, so there is no en passant capture; the pawn that arrived on b3 can still be captured normally, diagonally, by a black pawn on a4 or on c4.</p>\n<p><strong>Consequences</strong> (these are not new rules):</p>\n<ul><li>A check given by a battery cannot be answered by interposing a piece. It is answered by moving the king, or by capturing a pawn so that the king's square is no longer attacked.</li><li>Whoever captures the lead pawn of a battery of three lands on the square attacked by the two remaining pawns. The opposing king therefore cannot capture it.</li><li>The lead pawn of a battery of two is not defended by the rear pawn.</li></ul>\n<h3>6. End of the game</h3>\n<p id=\"r6.1\"><span class=\"n\">6.1.</span> <strong>Victory.</strong> A player wins when he or she:</p>\n<ul><li>a) gives checkmate: the opponent's king is in check and the opponent has no legal move;</li><li>b) stalemates the opponent: the opponent's king is not in check and the opponent has no legal move (unlike chess, stalemate is not a draw);</li><li>c) brings one of his or her own pawns to the last rank, alone or as the lead pawn of a block advance. The game ends at once.</li></ul>\n<p>The moves of exception 3.4 count as legal moves: a player whose king is in check but who can bring a pawn to the last rank is not checkmated, and wins with that move.</p>\n<p>In short, for a) and b): the player to move who has no legal move loses.</p>\n<p id=\"r6.2\"><span class=\"n\">6.2.</span> <strong>Capture of the king.</strong> It never happens: the only move that can leave one's own king in check (3.4) ends the game.</p>\n<p id=\"r6.3\"><span class=\"n\">6.3.</span> <strong>Draw.</strong> As in chess: agreement between the players, threefold repetition of the position, 50 consecutive moves by each side without a capture and without a pawn move, a position in which neither side can win any more (for example king against king).</p>\n<h3>7. Points to be confirmed</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>[D11] Double step of the pawns on the third and sixth rank.</strong> Assumed: the double step is available only from the second rank (White) and from the seventh (Black), so the three advanced pawns do not have it. A pawn that stayed on the second rank keeps it, if the two squares in front become empty.</li><li><strong>[D12] Hitch onto the last rank.</strong> Assumed: it is allowed and wins, like any other arrival of a pawn. Typical case: two pawns side by side on the seventh rank, with the square in front of each occupied, and one of them steps diagonally in front of the other.</li><li><strong>[D13] Hitch and en passant capture on the same square.</strong> Assumed: if the diagonal square is the en passant square, the move is always the capture. The case also requires a fellow pawn next to that square and is very rare.</li></ul>";
